<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Bind typed parameters, use omop_study and shortcuts, then derive and filter columns without bringing rows to your computer.
</div>

This chapter uses the public synthetic Synthea27Nj numeric connection from [Getting started](book-00-start.html).

**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run its sections in order. Each chapter starts from a fresh practice connection;
all the outputs below come from real dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.4 calls.
The numbers describe teaching data, not clinical findings.

## Start this chapter

Start a fresh R session in the `book-work` directory created in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#get-the-book-helpers). Run this setup first:

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
practice <- if (book_driver == "DSLite") book_practice("numeric") else book_connect("numeric")
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

The `site` connection is ready. The shared `connection.R` selects DSLite or Opal and resolves the resource names.

## Describe the setting as a parameter

A *parameter* is a named, typed placeholder in a study definition. It lets you
reuse a recipe without editing its logic. It is not R code or SQL text.

```{r parameter}
setting <- omop_param("setting", type = "concept_set")
class(setting)
```

This parameter accepts concept sets. A separate date parameter would declare a
date type; give each changing input its own appropriate type.

## Build a template

```{r template}
template <- omop_study(omop_event("visit_occurrence", concepts = setting),
  outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
    prior_visits = omop_count("visit_occurrence", window = omop_window(-365, -1)),
    age = omop_age()))
class(template)
```

`omop_study()` returns an ordinary recipe. It creates an indexed population and
baseline output. We explicitly exclude day zero from the prior-visit count.
The unbound template cannot execute until its required setting is supplied.

## Bind and inspect

```{r bind}
outpatient <- recipe_bind(template, setting = 9202L)
stopifnot(recipe_explain(outpatient, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
recipe_explain(outpatient)$selected
```

Binding fills the placeholder while preserving the template. Concept 9202 is the
outpatient visit setting, not the record's provenance type.

```{r run}
print(recipe_execute(outpatient, out = c(baseline = "D_param_outpatient"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_param_outpatient$age", datasources = conns)
```

The server computes annual age (index year minus birth year) at the first outpatient visit. The analyst
receives only the approved summary.

## Read the simple-layer defaults

```{r defaults}
print(outpatient)
```

The study's default index selection is `first`. The population uses the default
coverage policy; all requirements are combined with AND. Baseline has episode
rows. For survival, supply outcomes and an explicit follow-up definition. For
example, `follow_up = list(start = 1, end = 365, censor = c("observation_end",
"death"))` describes a year after entry, ending sooner at a listed censor.
The [output chapter](book-08-outputs.html) executes the corresponding native survival format.

## Shortcuts expand into ordinary variables

Use a shortcut when its defaults match your question; use an explicit reducer
when you need more control. `before = 365` includes day zero, while `after = 365`
starts at day one. Without either, most shortcuts use all history through entry.

```{r shortcut}
shortcuts <- omop_study(omop_event("measurement", 3000963L), outputs = "baseline",
  baseline = list(ever = omop_ever("measurement", 3000963L, before = 365),
    count = omop_count("measurement", 3000963L, before = 365),
    value = omop_value_at_index("measurement", 3000963L,
      tolerance = c(-30, 0), unit = 8713L)))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(shortcuts, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(shortcuts, out = c(baseline = "D_shortcuts"), conns = conns))
```
```{r shortcut-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_shortcuts$ever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
```

Everyone has ever = 1 because the index hemoglobin measurement itself is included. To ask about
measurements strictly before entry use `window = omop_window(-365, -1)` instead.
First/last/mean/min/max-value shortcuts use the same event and window model.
`omop_history()` bundles named ever variables; it does not define a new language.

## What the mutate-v1 contract means

`ds.omop.mutate()` creates or replaces columns on a private `omop.table`.
Although its notation looks like R, the client sends a **mutate-v1** data
request: the server validates a closed grammar and column types. It never runs
arbitrary R code. Bare names refer to columns, including earlier assignments in
the same call; literal numbers, strings, booleans and missing values are allowed.

The grammar supports `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, comparisons of a column with a literal,
`ifelse`, `case_when`, `coalesce`, and `cut` with public breaks. Date subtraction
returns days; adding an integer to a date returns a date. Division by zero or
nonfinite arithmetic gives missing values. Person, episode and set keys and
other protected columns cannot be read or written; class-1 source names are
also refused. [Privacy](book-12-privacy.html) explains those protections.

## Prepare weight and height at the same date

BMI is weight in kilograms divided by squared height in metres. Index each
person at their first weight measurement, then select weight and height on
that date. We require the recorded kg and cm units; we do not combine unrelated
latest readings or silently substitute a height from another day.

```{r body-inputs}
weight_event <- omop_event("measurement", 3025315L)
body <- omop_study(weight_event, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  weight_kg = omop_value_at_index("measurement", 3025315L,
    tolerance = c(0, 0), unit = 9529L),
  height_cm = omop_value_at_index("measurement", 3036277L,
    tolerance = c(0, 0), unit = 8582L),
  age = omop_age_at(omop_anchor(weight_event)),
  first_date = omop_date(omop_anchor(weight_event, which = "first")),
  last_date = omop_date(omop_anchor(weight_event, which = "last"))))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(body, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(body, out = c(baseline = "D_body"), conns = conns))
```

These are synthetic Synthea measurements, including children. Calculated BMI
is a numeric teaching example, not an adult clinical classification.

## Create a new object

```{r bmi-new}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_body", height_m = height_cm / 100,
  bmi = weight_kg / (height_m * height_m), newobj = "D_bmi", conns = conns))
bmi_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_bmi$bmi", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(bmi_result))
```

The second assignment uses the newly created `height_m`. A missing input gives
a missing BMI. `newobj = "D_bmi"` preserves `D_body`; neither table is downloaded.

```{r new-object-check}
body_columns <- dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_body", datasources = conns)
bmi_columns <- dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_bmi", datasources = conns)
c(original_has_bmi = "bmi" %in% body_columns$site,
  new_has_bmi = "bmi" %in% bmi_columns$site)
```

The original has no BMI column, and the new object does.

## Group ages with public breaks

Choose boundaries from the study design, not from private minimum/maximum ages.
These broad age bands are for teaching. `right = FALSE` includes the lower bound
and excludes the upper bound; ages outside the range remain missing.

```{r age-bands}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", age_group = cut(age, c(0, 18, 121),
  labels = c("under 18", "18 to 120"), right = FALSE),
  newobj = "D_grouped", conns = conns))
age_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_grouped$age_group",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(age_counts))
```

`cut` produces text labels in this grammar, not an ordered R factor. The table
may display them alphabetically. [Chapter 15](book-15-labs.html) shows how to
assign an explicit factor level sequence for modelling and display.

**Exercise:** create an adult flag using the same lower boundary of 18.

<details><summary>Solution — an age recode</summary>

```{r adult-solution}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_grouped", adult = ifelse(age >= 18, "yes", "no"),
  newobj = "D_adults", conns = conns))
adult_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_adults$adult", useNA = "always",
  datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(adult_counts))
```

A missing age stays missing rather than becoming “no”. The adult count combines
the 18-to-120 band; the under-18 band becomes “no”.

</details>

## Subtract dates on the server

```{r date-difference}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", span_days = last_date - first_date,
  newobj = "D_span", conns = conns))
span_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_span$span_days", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(span_result))
```

This is elapsed time between first and last recorded weights, in days. It is
not age or duration under continuous observation. Exact dates stay private.

**Exercise:** express that duration in 365-day years, retaining the days column.

<details><summary>Solution — convert a duration</summary>

```{r duration-solution}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_span", span_years = span_days / 365,
  newobj = "D_years", conns = conns))
years_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_years$span_years", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(years_result))
```

Each summary is the day summary divided by 365. These are fixed-length units,
not completed birthdays; use `omop_age_at` for completed age.

</details>

## Recode with case_when

Cases are checked in order. The first true condition wins. In mutate-v1, an
unknown condition gives a missing result immediately, rather than falling
through to the default. Handle missingness explicitly when your study needs it.

```{r case-recode}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", life_stage = case_when(
  is.na(age) ~ "unknown", age < 5 ~ "young child",
  age < 18 ~ "older child", .default = "adult"),
  newobj = "D_stage", conns = conns))
stage_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_stage$life_stage",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(stage_counts))
```

The declared default applies only after the preceding conditions are false.
This recode distinguishes unknown age from a known age; it does not impute age.

**Exercise:** recode both child labels to “no”, and the adult label to “yes”.

<details><summary>Solution — recode a derived category</summary>

```{r recode-solution}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_stage", adult = case_when(
  life_stage %in% c("young child", "older child") ~ "no",
  life_stage == "unknown" ~ NA,
  .default = "yes"), newobj = "D_recoded", conns = conns))
recode_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_recoded$adult", useNA = "always",
  datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(recode_counts))
```

These counts agree with the earlier adult flag. Here the synthetic source has
complete ages, so the missingness branch introduces no extra category count.

</details>

## Replace an object in place

Use the same name for `x` and `newobj` to replace that server object. Omitting
`newobj` instead generates a fresh name; it does not mean “in place”.

```{r in-place}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", height_m = height_cm / 100,
  bmi = weight_kg / (height_m * height_m), bmi_copy = bmi,
  newobj = "D_bmi", conns = conns))
before_columns <- dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_bmi", datasources = conns)
before_bmi <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_bmi$bmi", datasources = conns)
c(copy_added = "bmi_copy" %in% before_columns$site,
  bmi_unchanged = identical(bmi_result, before_bmi))
```

The replacement adds `bmi_copy` while preserving BMI. The client keeps a rollback
copy until all participating sites succeed, restoring originals if assignment
fails. This single-site practice example verifies that failure leaves the table
usable; it does not simulate a network outage.

## Read the refusal messages

The following failures are intentional. First try an unknown function:

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r unknown-function}
unknown_message <- tryCatch({
  ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", bmi = sqrt(bmi), newobj = "D_bmi", conns = conns)
  "Unexpected success"
}, error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
cat(unknown_message, "\n")
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

`sqrt` is outside the closed grammar and is refused by the client before a
request can alter the table. Use supported arithmetic or a documented analysis
function; arbitrary R functions cannot be sent through mutate.

Next try to copy a protected key. The server refuses this column reference:

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r protected-read, warning=FALSE}
key_message <- tryCatch({
  ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", copied = person_id,
    newobj = "D_key_copy", conns = conns)
  "Unexpected success"
}, error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
cat(key_message, "\n")
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

A class-1 source name is refused even when used as the name of a new column:

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r blocked-column, warning=FALSE}
blocked_message <- tryCatch({
  ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", person_source_value = "label",
    newobj = "D_blocked_copy", conns = conns)
  "Unexpected success"
}, error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
cat(blocked_message, "\n")
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

This is a blocked source/assignment name, not a request to alter its protection.
Use descriptive study-variable names for allowed derived values.

Finally request an ordinary BMI change followed by a protected-key overwrite
in the same in-place call. The whole request must fail:

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r failed-in-place, warning=FALSE}
rollback_message <- tryCatch({
  ds.omop.mutate("D_bmi", bmi = bmi + 1, person_id = "replacement",
    newobj = "D_bmi", conns = conns)
  "Unexpected success"
}, error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
cat(rollback_message, "\n")
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

The protected-identifier message applies to person, episode and set keys and
any columns marked protected by the server. It prohibits both reads and writes.
Renaming a key is not an allowed transformation.

```{r rollback-check}
after_columns <- dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_bmi", datasources = conns)
after_bmi <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_bmi$bmi", datasources = conns)
c(columns_preserved = identical(before_columns, after_columns),
  bmi_preserved = identical(before_bmi, after_bmi),
  refused_copies_absent = !any(c("D_key_copy", "D_blocked_copy") %in%
    DSI::datashield.symbols(conns)$site))
```

All checks are true. Even the earlier `bmi + 1` assignment was not committed.
These released checks establish schema and summary stability; the book's
synthetic-source verification additionally checks equality of the entire table.

## Filter using a predicate

A *predicate* is a condition. Only TRUE rows survive; missing conditions are
dropped. This retains adults without using a key to target a known person.

```{r filter}
print(ds.omop.filter("D_bmi", expr = omop_where(age >= 18),
  newobj = "D_adult_bmi", conns = conns))
filtered_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_adult_bmi$bmi", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(filtered_result))
```

The subset meets the distinct-person release threshold in this practice data.
It remains a private table. A small or empty final subset can be refused, as
[chapter 12](book-12-privacy.html) demonstrates.

**Exercise:** keep the adult category from `D_stage` with the same filter grammar.

<details><summary>Solution — filter a text category</summary>

```{r filter-solution}
print(ds.omop.filter("D_stage", expr = omop_where(life_stage == "adult"),
  newobj = "D_working_age", conns = conns))
working_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_working_age$bmi", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(working_result))
```

This category means ages 18 and over, so its summary agrees with the preceding
filter. The earlier missingness case ensures an unknown age is not called adult.

</details>

## Recap

Parameters change declared inputs, shortcuts express common native recipes, and mutate/filter operate privately on assigned tables. Each final object still passes publication checks.

## Try it

Bind the same template to inpatient visits and summarise the prior-visit count. Do not alter the outpatient template in place.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r solution}
inpatient <- recipe_bind(template, setting = 9201L)
stopifnot(recipe_explain(inpatient, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(inpatient, out = c(baseline = "D_param_inpatient"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_param_inpatient$prior_visits", datasources = conns)
```
Each binding is a separate recipe. Its own index changes which earlier records fall into the lookback window. A numeric summary suits these varied counts; a categorical table can be refused when it creates too many distinct levels in a small population.

</details>
